From: local
Subject: [PATCH] app: allow cropping the X11 fallback capture to a region

When no ScreenCast portal is available (e.g. an XFCE/X11 session, where
xdg-desktop-portal-gnome cannot provide screencasting because Mutter is
not running), gnome-network-displays falls back to a bare ximagesrc that
grabs the whole root window, so the sink can only ever mirror.

Honour $GND_X11_REGION ("WxH+X+Y") to crop that capture to a sub-region
of the root window. Combined with a virtual display (evdi, or any real
second output), this gives a real extended display over Miracast instead
of a mirror.

$GND_X11_REGION_FILE names a file that is re-read once a second while
streaming. ximagesrc reads startx/starty/endx/endy on every frame, so the
capture can be moved to follow a display the user repositions in arandr
or xfce4-display-settings, with no interruption to the session. Only the
origin can move that way: the caps are negotiated once, so a region of a
different size is refused rather than passed on as a corrupt stream.

Moving a display can also resize the X root window, and that needs three
further things to stay usable:

ximagesrc resets its crop to the full screen whenever a resize leaves the
old crop overshooting the root window (gst_ximage_src_get_caps), so the
poll compares against the element's live properties, not just the previous
file contents, and re-asserts the region when it sees that.

The capture is scaled to a fixed size before intervideosink, so what
crosses the intervideosink/intervideosrc bridge never changes size. That
pair negotiates caps once, and letting the reset above change them wedges
it: the stream keeps flowing at full bitrate while the sink shows black
for the rest of the session.

Even with both of those the pipeline does not fully recover -- the picture
comes back but is unstable -- so a detected reset also tears the session
down and re-connects to the same sink. The sink reports DISCONNECTED
before its P2P group has finished going away and starting into that fails,
so the reconnect waits for it to settle and retries a few times.
$GND_X11_RECONNECT=0 disables it.

The connect itself is handed to an idle rather than run from the timer:
it repopulates the sink list models and switches stack pages, and doing
that with GTK layout still pending crashes inside gtk_viewport_scroll_to
after a few reconnects.

Three further things the reconnect needs to be safe:

A discovered sink is an NdMetaSink wrapping whichever provider currently
offers it, and a teardown can leave it with no backing sink until the peer
is re-discovered. Starting a stream on it in that state asserts in
nd_meta_sink_sink_start_stream, so wait for it to become connectable and
give up after a minute rather than aborting.

Every connect builds a new capture bin with its own region watcher, and
the bins from previous connects outlive their session. A stale watcher
whose element had been reset kept asking for reconnects forever, so a
watcher retires itself once it is no longer the current capture element.

Reconnects are rate limited: if the capture looks lost again within a
minute of one, something other than a one-off resize is wrong and cycling
the session would only make it worse.

XDamage is deliberately left at its default (enabled). Forcing it off
here made the encoded stream flicker badly and smear the cursor on a real
Miracast sink, while an identical uncropped capture was clean; the full
frame reads interact badly with the rest of the pipeline. $GND_X11_DAMAGE=0
disables it for anyone who wants to experiment.
--- a/src/app/nd-window.c
--- a/src/app/nd-window.c
+++ b/src/app/nd-window.c
@@ -16,6 +16,8 @@
